#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/id #uuid "fbe81109-2a56-5dd3-bab5-4ac5cc6ca860" :question/math-academy-id "e-12458" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z) =  x-y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=2$ and the cylinder $x^2+z^2=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) =   x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -1 &=\\lambda \\\\ -1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=-1 \\\\ \\mu z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu$ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $z= 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2, 0, 0), \\qquad (-2, 4, 0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2, 0, 0 )$ | $(-2, 4, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2, 4, 0),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171717 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/id #uuid "b8496dce-3f8b-5516-874d-17d82cfb41b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12459" :question/problem "Suppose we want to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xz+y^2$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-z=2$ and $x^2+y^2+2z^2=3$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2x⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ x-z &=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 2xz+y^2\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+2z^2=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2z,\\,2y,\\,2x⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,4z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are $2y,\\:$ $-1,\\:$ and $4z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6dc64a5-a90d-5dd1-a93d-97925fd04228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3y\\,$ and $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363481} {:answer/id #uuid "09611345-5534-57f8-ae82-2cede0b9efb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y\\,$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363482} {:answer/id #uuid "1c39a77f-8bbe-502b-85b2-5cb5a5263132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363483} {:answer/id #uuid "381dc42b-11bf-512a-911f-3e01e2a323be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3{y}^{2}\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363484} {:answer/id #uuid "70992b12-a416-5e5c-aa21-4b0454c3e299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}^{3}\\,$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c39a77f-8bbe-502b-85b2-5cb5a5263132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363483} :answer-field/id #uuid "a753b91d-1a9d-5b6d-831f-daf49b3ab843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "663a6461-43e7-5235-b081-b1415045b679" :question/math-academy-id "q-162808" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-y+z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+z=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-y+z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+z=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b77cdb3-d7ff-584b-823c-bacf0508544b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363488} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3ef2fc-3c76-5a57-8c8b-618e09709f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363489} {:answer/id #uuid "3123d726-b828-5084-8aba-af5d0eec4f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $-z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363490} {:answer/id #uuid "26359364-b89e-59ac-a45f-72a93507aa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2z$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363491} {:answer/id #uuid "34c4b7fe-3b94-5cc5-aaa9-7fc9f7c472c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ and $-y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26359364-b89e-59ac-a45f-72a93507aa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2z$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a90c27af-1043-5978-9fc0-31ca8ca43adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3568a5f0-4e3e-54dc-8dd0-05da08b2ea24" :question/math-academy-id "q-162810" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-yz$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y+{z}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+z=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,-y⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,\\,⟩ \\\\ y+{z}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-yz\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+z=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48267671-3c22-599a-906d-15947ab84541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y,\\,$ $2,\\,$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493513} {:answer/id #uuid "5850d96f-cd3a-52af-b926-0eb94f43b034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x,\\,$ $-1,\\,$ and $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493527} {:answer/id #uuid "637782f8-5060-58aa-b1ac-cd0c5892b07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4y,\\,$ $2z,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493540} {:answer/id #uuid "9765660b-c840-5458-b02f-de5fbb17e64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4y,\\,$ $1,\\,$ and $-2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493631} {:answer/id #uuid "ee237b50-6e80-527a-87ec-af3f5a3a9282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2z,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "637782f8-5060-58aa-b1ac-cd0c5892b07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4y,\\,$ $2z,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493540} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab363583-7f08-5d8c-81f8-2a64b51d7010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d5803ec-f126-5445-94a3-4045259deba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162814" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ and $2x-y+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-2z⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} &=4 \\\\ 2x-y+{z}^{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=2x-y+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c8a2b1-da85-5994-b78c-c9e95612a9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493431} {:answer/id #uuid "7d084b5c-a828-5df4-9946-4103e1f34b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $8z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493584} {:answer/id #uuid "95cf3ed2-0d12-5a39-bf5c-97ada95a469b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2y,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and ${z}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493624} {:answer/id #uuid "df044209-61e8-50cd-9db7-ccaaa780982c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2,\\,$ $1,\\,$ and $4z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493743} {:answer/id #uuid "e107cf08-1118-512e-98ba-d7f6aff904ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}^{2},\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $4{z}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d084b5c-a828-5df4-9946-4103e1f34b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $8z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493584} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f22b591-30cd-52f7-a24e-bb816c9f1346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aa1e55d-7de9-53b4-a8d5-5393a30ab5e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162816" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨3,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,\\,2y,\\,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24445614-cbdd-51e8-9c4a-2bb44029ae1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411699} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6381f4-a213-5d2b-a1cc-2cec4867e43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411718} {:answer/id #uuid "9a843324-4732-5b29-8d8d-9057bdddcade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411760} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc80792-2d44-5042-bdf5-ee15cb21d305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411778} {:answer/id #uuid "f4841998-b430-5860-99e4-67399dd743f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a6381f4-a213-5d2b-a1cc-2cec4867e43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411718} :answer-field/id #uuid "a18338f5-cccd-53a6-b949-c33ab13e3ef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4bd89e0-666c-5b6d-a3d4-7d170f1093d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162818" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x-2y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=2$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x-2y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -2 &=\\lambda \\\\ -2 &=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=-2 \\\\ \\mu z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 1+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 1+y & =2 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -1+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -1+y & =2 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,0),\\,(-1,3,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,0)$ | $(-1,3,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,3,0),$ and its  minimum value is $-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee41277-7935-51f1-85bc-15927e8fe4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389369} {:answer/id #uuid "174eadcd-7d74-5372-a6ec-7880566d72a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389370} {:answer/id #uuid "6962c134-0174-5b89-8601-d57405b065ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389371} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9dabf2-ce85-58ec-937f-7e2a0a8245d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389372} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed5aec2-6866-596e-9035-82e42640cccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "174eadcd-7d74-5372-a6ec-7880566d72a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389370} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3e28af-f22d-50e3-abe7-b3442baab90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27d6b0fc-4f5c-5b3d-9ebf-da8e41d423eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162820" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $y-z=1$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y-z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2\\mu x \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda \\\\ -1 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=1 \\\\ \\mu z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$ into the first constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-z & =1 \\\\ y-0 & =1 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute  $z=0$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,1,0),\\,(-2,1,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,1,0)$ | $(-2,1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $9$ | $-7$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,1,0),$ and its maximum value is $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0005042c-e337-5a0c-a1db-b063bfe254a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493391} {:answer/id #uuid "18c4cb35-744c-5bb2-be59-05606fbe5ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493433} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8fefed-a005-5293-b27d-4d1ec085f9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493633} {:answer/id #uuid "b3adee24-018e-57fe-bcb0-1019592eb22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493682} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e91317-8af1-5dfd-a4d3-8c380cd7d717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e91317-8af1-5dfd-a4d3-8c380cd7d717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493684} :answer-field/id #uuid "38401504-2977-5fc3-a7df-aed6a5b7e6b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90c2cf43-9c0a-5736-b63a-0ceceb8b2660" :question/math-academy-id "q-162893" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=0$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2 &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ 2+(-1)+z & =0 \\\\ 1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ -2+1+z & =0 \\\\ -1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,-1,-1),\\,(-2,1,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,-1,-1)$ | $(-2,1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $5$ | $-5$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5faa12-b9d6-5195-beb4-d5b87ef6c5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363509} {:answer/id #uuid "b8256f54-aaa6-5303-b548-a62b52de9dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363510} {:answer/id #uuid "03053988-f40f-50bf-bd44-429f31f885a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363511} {:answer/id #uuid "b960a95a-dad2-53b9-a7ba-bea3e7f53c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363512} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0556de-84ea-59dd-914f-d1f9b6267c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b960a95a-dad2-53b9-a7ba-bea3e7f53c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363512} :answer-field/id #uuid "730045aa-92dc-5b12-8a88-20b951ca5be6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "886a40d4-98a6-535f-be10-c412266e6fef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162895" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3x+2y+z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+2y+z=1$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3x+2y+z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+2y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,2,1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,4y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3 &=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda +4\\mu y \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{4\\mu } \\\\ \\mu y &=0 \\\\ \\lambda &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the second equation above, we must have $y=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $y=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+2(0)+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+2(0)+z & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{3}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ and its  minimum value is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35746dd5-60c7-5b7c-9936-da62f086bab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512218} {:answer/id #uuid "88b5f66c-f0e6-5312-b69e-25574fe5c5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512418} {:answer/id #uuid "da111704-a982-535c-b453-0caf2ce1247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512619} {:answer/id #uuid "f07b1405-5db5-53fd-92b4-e9d3aa736944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512682} {:answer/id #uuid "f4740cdc-4247-561b-a719-1d4642b2932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4740cdc-4247-561b-a719-1d4642b2932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512691} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02d209a-9adb-5732-8214-dc18cb0b1819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3250257a-998e-56c2-b2b7-fdd8d8e6863b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162896" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+4z=4$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+4z=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,4⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda \\\\ -2 &=4\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\mu x &=0 \\\\ \\lambda &=1 \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the first equation above, we must have $x=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(1) & =4 \\\\ y+4 & =4 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(-1) & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =4 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0,1),\\,(0,8,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(0,0,1)$ | $(0,8,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,8,-1),$ and its  maximum value is $10.$"}]}
